NOTE: This was the manual that shipped with V3.0, and was the only copy of
the manual I still have after uncounted migrations from the original '486
machine i developed this software on, to the relatively new machine i'm
currently using. This manual doesn't outline the use of the SVGA patterns,
but they're much the same as the VGA patterns in usage. I did begin making
some changes in the first part of the manual, but it'll be a while before I
complete it.

If you have further questions, email me at unclejed613c@yahoo.com






































VIDEO TEST BENCH V4.5

INTRODUCTION:
   VIDEO TEST BENCH V4.5 IS A COMPREHENSIVE TEST PATTERN GENERATOR PROGRAM
   WITH MONO, CGA, EGA,VGA,AND SVGA TEST PATTERNS. I HOPE THAT THIS PROGRAM
   PROVES AS USEFUL TO YOU AS IT DOES TO ME FOR TROUBLESHOOTING AND ALIGNING
   COMPUTER MONITORS. As for the reason I wrote it, the company that I
   work for couldn't afford a video test generator (about $3000) when I
   began working for them. At my previous job, I had used one extensively.
   But at my new job, the company did mostly computer assembly, setup
   and repair, and not much monitor work. The existing software was
   slow, cumbersome, didn't make proper use of video modes, and had very few
   useable test patterns. It was based on a popular DATABASE MANAGEMENT
   SYSTEM, with a totally bizarre video command set. I began to write
   a few simple, but useful patterns in (much quicker and friendly) QBASIC
   and compiled them as .EXE files. Soon I had enough patterns that I
   no longer missed having a test generator. But hard drive space began
   to catch up to me, and I put all of the separate patterns "under one
   roof" along with their own menu.

GENERAL INFO AND NOTES
   1> Most of the test patterns are referenced for FULL BRIGHTNESS AND
      EITHER CENTER CONTRAST (for mono, cga, and ega) OR FULL CONTRAST
      (for vga) unless otherwise indicated.

   2> To simplify equipment requirements, I suggest a Trident or Oak
      video card with both analog and TTL outputs. These cards generally
      can run mono, cga, ega and vga modes. The mode selector DIP switches
      can be run "remote" with a piece of ribbon cable and a remote switch
      box.

   3> SVGA was added in 1996

   4> The program has been tested with DOS versions 3.3, 5.0, and 6.22.
      It also works with the "DOS" that came with Win9x, but not with
      any hardware abstraced OS (Win@k, WinXP, etc...).
      Hardware requirements are- IBM compatible with at least 256 k
      of memory, and a video card appropriate for the monitor under test.
      Obviously, the faster the pc, the better. I've used this program
      on a '486 DX/50 and it draws lightning fast, but economically
      a '286 is probably the best choice between speed and cost.
      A '286 laptop is probably a good idea for a field tester, since
      it's portable and easy to set up.

   5> Some patterns (namely the VGA RGBI and the 64 STEP GRAY SCALE) take
      from 20 to 60 seconds to draw on an 8088 or 8086 or a slow (6 or 8 Mhz)
      '286.

   6> Unlike prior versions of VIDEO TEST BENCH, all patterns and menus can
      be exited with the <ESC> key. The VGA TEST LOOP and the MODE SWITCHING
      test patterns do not exit immediately. These two patterns each have
      a single exit point, and you may have to wait for the loop to run it's
      course.

   7> When setting up a vga card, be certain that the card is configured
      for COLOR VGA and not MONO VGA. VIDEO TEST BENCH will return an
      error message (Illegal function call) if a card set up for MONO VGA
      is used. Mono vga monitors can be tested with the card in COLOR mode.
      The GREEN video line is used to drive the monitor.

   8> The 200 LINE (400 LINE VGA) CROSSHATCH) pattern only allows
      the colors to be changed when a CGA monitor is being tested.
      The commands for the crosshatch pattern are: R red, G green, B blue,
      M magenta, C cyan, Y yellow, W white, Z reverse video (ega,vga),
      and X or <ESC> to exit.

   9> VTB 4.5 will run from Windows 3.1, 95, and 98, but make sure you have
      at least 4 meg of memory. Otherwise Windows may corrupt the existing
      test pattern if you swap out to another application and back
      to VTB 4.5. Again, VTB will not run on OS's that use hardware
      abstraction (i.e. Windows NT, 2000, XP, Vista and 7).

  10> Where a particular test pattern has particular relevance to the use
      of a oscilloscope, I have added notes to explain the use of an
      oscilloscope with that particular test pattern. For VGA and SVGA
      monitors, an oscilloscope with a minimum of 100MHz bandwidth is
      recommended for troubleshooting video amp problems, as the pixel
      clock for some modes is about 100MHz. There is some leeway with
      analog scopes, but with a digital scope, the published bandwidth
      is usually a hard limit (Nyquist limit) at half the sample rate.


 ******  SOME NOTES ADDED 19NOV26 ******



ADDITIONS AND CHANGES FOR VERSION 3.0:
   1> VTB 3.0 now autodetects the video card and in most cases brings
      the program up in the appropriate menu (MONO,CGA,EGA,VGA).
      The only case i have so far discovered so far where the autodetect
      doesn't function has been a Compaq Portable with the CGA compatible
      MONO/COLOR card. In such a case, VTB 3.0 defaults to the MAIN MENU.
      VTB 3.0  will autodetect the mode to which an OAK 037 or TRIDENT
      VGA card has been set with DIP switches.

   2> VTB's PURITY test has been changed. Instead of stepping through
      red, green, blue, and white screens, the commands R, G, B, W, and
      <ESC> have been implemented in the same manner as the crosshatch
      patterns. Also the commands R, G, B, M, C, Y, and <ESC> have
      been implemented in the STANDARD LUMINANCE WINDOWS pattern.

   3> The PHOSPHOR IONIZATION pattern now times out the necessary
      3 minutes, but alas does not allow the 3 minute countdown to be
      aborted. I usually use the time to either check other monitors
      already burning in, or take a 3 minute break.

   4> VTB 3.0 includes test loops for CGA, EGA, and MONO monitors.

   5> VTB 3.0 includes 2 new test patterns in the VGA COLOR BAR MENU.
      RGB STRIPES and GREY SCALE REFERENCE.



THE MENUS AND THEIR TEST PATTERNS:
 1> CGA MENU
    1> COLOR BARS   This pattern consists of 16 color bars displaying the
                    standard IBM color set. This pattern is useful for
                    checking overall color balance, intermittant video
                    cables, brightness and contrast settings, and TEXT
                    SWITCH operation on CGA monitors.

    2> CENTERING    For adjusting V and H centering. The pattern consists
                    of a screen size box woth two diagonal lines through it,
                    and two small circles in the center. The two circles
                    represent centering errors of 5% for the small circle,
                    and 10% for the larger one. Centering error is estimated
                    by placing two strings from corner to corner diagonally
                    across the face of the tube and observing the point
                    at which the strings intersect. If they intersect
                    within the small circle, the centering is acceptable.

    3> HV REGULATION  This pattern has a box around the edge of the screen,
                    and a slightly smaller solid block which appears and
                    disappears. As the block appears and disappears, the
                    box around the outside of the screen should not shift
                    more than half the width of a text line.

    4> CROSSHATCH   For adjusting V HGT, V LIN, H WIDTH, PINCUSHION, &
                    CONVERGENCE. Commands are <R> red, <G> green,
                    <B> blue, <M> magenta, <C> cyan, <Y> yellow,
                    <W> white, <X> exit. The most common use for the
                    color switching feature is magenta lines for
                    R-B convergence, and yellow lines for R-G convergence,
                    then white lines to check overall convergence and
                    other adjustments. Convergence adjustments can be
                    tricky, and may take several passes to get it right.
                    Convergence adjustments take time, patience and
                    practice. Usually, the center of the screen should be
                    almost perfect, and some (about a half of a pixel width)
                    misconvergence at the edges usually are acceptable.

    5> FOCUS        Adjust static focus (on the flyback or a large ceramic
                    pot near it) for clearly defined pixels in the center
                    of the screen. If there is a dynamic focus pot, it is
                    for the edges of the screen.

    6> PURITY       Used for checking and adjusting purity. Be sure to
                    degauss the tube first, and with tubes larger than
                    15" orient the tube to face north or south (to minimize
                    the effects of the earth's magnetic field). Then, if
                    the tube still displays discoloration on any of the
                    screens in the PURITY pattern, adjust the purity
                    magnets on the neck of the tube. If the purity
                    cannot be corrected by these methods, then the
                    deflection yoke may need to be repositioned (not
                    a job for the inexperienced tech).

    7> MULTIBURST  This pattern is basically a frequency response test
                   for the video amplifiers. The closer the vertical stripes,
                   the higher the frequency. If the smallest stripes are
                   blurred together, or just look like a large gray stripe,
                   you may have a video problem. Usually this is caused
                   by leaky electrolytic capacitors on the video board.

    8> LUMINANCE WINDOW    I included this pattern for anyone who
                   uses a color/luminance probe.

    9> MAGENTA CROSSHATCH   DISCONTINUED.

    A> COLOR BALANCE   Prints 6 small blocks, red, green, and blue at
                   2 brightness levels. For adjusting the color balance
                   of CGA monitors. Turn brightness down until the
                   brightest blocks are barely visible. R,G,+B should
                   be equally visible.

    B> CGA TEST LOOP   Runs a series of CGA test patterns in a continuous
                   loop. The loop can be exited by pressing any key
                   and waiting for the loop to finish. The TEST LOOPs
                   are for "burn-in" testing of monitors. It is best
                   to burn-in a monitor for at least 72 hours on a
                   7 hour on, 1 hour off cycle. It is also a good idea
                   to set up a separate computer with a video distribution
                   box for monitor burn-in testing. A distribution box
                   for MONO, CGA, and EGA, would only require 1 TTL hex
                   buffer for each monitor. The details for an inexpensive
                   VGA distribution amplifier are available from me by
                   request.

2> EGA MENU
    1> COLOR BARS  Same as CGA COLOR BARS except in 640x350 mode.

    2> MODE SWITCH  Tests mode switching between 15.75 kHz (CGA) and
                   21 kHz (EGA).

    3> CROSSHATCH  640x350 line crosshatch pattern. Commands are
                   similar to CGA CROSSHATCH, <R> red, <G> green,
                   <B> blue, <M> magenta, <C> cyan, <Y> yellow,
                   <W> white, <Z> reverse video, <X> exit.

    4> FOCUS       Focus pattern for 640x350 line mode.

    5> MULTIBURST  Multiburst pattern for 640x350 line mode.
                   Since the horizontal sweep frequency is higher
                   than that of CGA, the pixel rate frequencies
                   are proportionally higher.

    6> RGBI PATTERN  Color bars of red, green, blue, and gray scale
                   for adjusting color balance of EGA monitors, and
                   for checking color matrix PAL chips.

    7> EGA TEST LOOP  A test loop for EGA monitor burn-in testing.
                   Contains EGA and CGA patterns to check both modes.

3> VGA MENU   Splits VGA patterns into 4 submenus:
  1> COLOR BAR MENU
  2> CROSSHATCH MENU
  3> MISC ADJ MENU
  4> VGA UTILITIES


  1> COLOR BAR MENU
    1> 16 COLOR    Same as CGA + EGA color bars except in 320x200 mode.

    2> 256 COLOR   Displays the complete 256 color default pallette in
                   320x200 mode.

    3> RGBI SCALE  Smooth intensity scales for red, green, blue, and
                   white. For checking overall color balance and video
                   gain linearity. Consists of 64 intensity steps
                   of R,G,B,+I.

    4> SATURATED COLORS Used for checking top-end linearity of video
                   amplifiers. Each color bar should be discernable
                   from the next and not appear washed out.

    5> 480 LINE BARS  Similar to the 16 COLOR pattern except in
                   640x480 line mode, and displayed split screen.

    6> NTSC BARS   Standard NTSC color bar pattern with a gray scale
                   strip. There are low level magenta and low level
                   blue squares beside the large white square.
                   The magenta and blue squares should be barely
                   visible with no back raster showing. This pattern
                   may also be useful for aligning VGA to NTSC broadcast
                   converters.

    7> COMBINED BARS  For checking overall video quality. Has 16 color
                   bar set, saturated color scale, and 16 step gray scale.

    8> 16 STEP GRAY SCALE  16 even gray steps, split screen. For adjusting
                   subbrightness, subcontrast, color balance, color
                   gain. Black ends should be black with no back raster,
                   and first gray bar should be barely visible.
                   Last white bar should have no discoloration after
                   pattern has been displayed at full brightness and
                   contrast for about 1 minute. Bars should be steel
                   gray with no tinges of other color.

    9> 64 STEP GRAY SCALE  64 even gray steps, split screen. For
                   checking video linearity. All steps should be
                   clearly visible, especially on the white end.
                   Some of the ones on the black end may be invisible
                   or next to impossible to see.

    A> RGB STRIPES   Another way of cross-checking color balance.
                   This pattern should appear as a grey screen from
                   about 5 feet away. Don't look too close, it will
                   make your eyes buggy.

    B> GREY SCALE REFERENCE   Displays a double 64 step grey scale and
                   a black box, and a white box. The  edge of the dark
                   end of the top grey scale should show up (in comparison
                   to the black part of the screen) all the way
                   until the next to last dark grey bar. The bright
                   end of the bottom grey scale should be discernable
                   from the white part of the screen until the next to
                   last light grey bar. Also the back raster should just
                   barely be visible.
          NOTE:    When using an oscilloscope on
                   the video circuits (especially on the CRT cathodes),
                   when the sweep is set to show a line of video,
                   this test pattern will draw a box on the scope with
                   an X through it. The arms of the X should be straight,
                   indicating good linearity of the video amps. If the lines
                   are curved, suspect dried out capacitors, or drifting
                   resistors causing nonlinear amplification of the video.

  2> CROSSHATCH MENU
    1> 350 LINE    Same pattern as the EGA crosshatch pattern.

    2> 400 LINE    Same pattern as the CGA 200 line pattern, except
                   that because of the way a vga card sets it's
                   color palette, the color commands do not work.

    3> 480 LINE    Reminiscent of the old TV "INDIAN HEAD" test
                   patterns used by early TV stations. Has "target"
                   circles, crosshairs with tickmarks, a mini set
                   of color bars, and focus fans. Commands are:
                   <R> red, <G> green, <B> blue, <M> magenta,
                   <C> cyan, <Y> yellow, <Z> reverse video, <X> exit.
                   VGA monitors may have as many as 3 vert size
                   controls, which is why i have included 3 patterns
                   here.

    4> CENTERING   Similar to the CGA centering except it's in
                   640x480 line mode

    5> 400LINE SPECIAL  320x200 graphics pattern for checking
                   overall picture quality.

    6> COLOR GRID  For checking convergence. Grid with red, green,
                   and blue segments. Segments should line up perfect
                   in the center of the screen with a maximum error
                   of one half pixel width at the edges.

  3> MISC ADJ MENU
    1> MULTIBURST  640x480 line version of the multiburst pattern.
                   The horizontal sweep frequency is 31.5 kHz, so
                   the maximum pixel rate is about 10 MHz, requiring
                   about 60-70 MHz bandwidth for accurate reproduction
                   of the video waveform.
          NOTE:    Problems with video amp frequency response can
                   easily be diagnosed with an oscilloscope with
                   this test pattern.

    2> PURITY      This purity pattern is actually the same subroutine
                   used for the CGA purity pattern. VGA monitors are
                   more susceptible to purity errors, especially 17"
                   and 19" monitors.

    3> HVREG       Same pattern as in the CGA menu. Generally VGA
                   monitors should have better HV regulation than
                   CGA and EGA monitors, but the less expensive
                   the monitor, the less safeguards are built in.

    4> COLOR BALANCE  A white luminance window. The content of
                   red, green, and blue are continuously variable
                   from 0 to 63. In the upper left hand corner are
                   3 indicators, red, green, and blue. The relative
                   positions of each indicator show the relative
                   amount of each color in the center window. The
                   commandkeys for each color work similar to the
                   channel up/down arrows on a TV remote control.
                   Red uses <E> for down, <R> for up. Green uses
                   <F> for down, <G> for up. Blue uses <V> for down,
                   <B> for up. Notice that the up/down keys are
                   next to each other. <X> or <ESC> to exit.
                   If you adjust the colors until the monitor
                   under test shows the window as white, you
                   will see the relative color balance errors
                   in the positions of the RGB indicators.

    5> TEXT        Fills the screen with text and extended ASCII
                   characters.

    6> COLOR CONTRAST  Standard color contrast pattern displaying
                   3 horizontal bars, each in 3 sections, with
                   primary colors on the outside, complementary
                   colors on the inside. The sections should have
                   clear, sharp borders, without appearing smeared
                   or washed out.

    7> VGA FOCUS #1  640x480 line focus pattern. Individual pixels
                   should be visible, sometimes with a moire`
                   (interference fringe) pattern on the screen.

    8> ANALOG ECHO  A vertival white line at the left edge of the
                   screen, with a gray background. Gray background
                   should appear solid, with no ghosts or shadows
                   of the white line. This test is useful for
                   checking video cables which may be changing
                   impedance due to an open ground or pinched
                   coax cables. Also may indicate improper
                   termination impedance in the video amp due to a
                   component failure.

    9> VIDEO AMP OVERLOAD  Horizontal R,G,B bars ending in the center
                   of the screen, followed by vertical white measurement
                   lines. Bars should stop AT the first measurement
                   line, and not bleed over it. Excessive gain in a
                   video amp, causing saturation is the source of
                   most bleed over. Other causes may be defective
                   electrolytic coupling or bypass caps.
           NOTE:   An oscilloscope can be helpful in determining
                   the cause of smearing. Sometimes it's a saturated
                   amplifier that's slow to respond, or it may be
                   some oscillation caused by dried out capacitors.

    A> VGA FOCUS #2  A more complex focus pattern. A small 4 segment
                   target should be clearly defined at the center
                   of the screen with most of the rest of the pattern
                   also clear.

    B> VGA FOCUS #3  5 small target patterns. The 4 segment target in
                   each should be in focus and clearly defined.

    C> DYNAMIC HV REG  A green box with a large white horizontal bar.
                   The box should not appear to be deformed more
                   than 1/4 the width of a text character, and the
                   white bar should appear to be rectangular, not
                   trapezoidal (i.e. should have square corners).
                   Most cheaper monitors fail this test.

    D> STANDARD LUM WINDOWS  5 white squares for use with a luminance
                   probe. Also uses R, G, B, M, C, Y, W, Z, and <ESC>
                   commands as in the 480 LINE CROSSHATCH pattern.
                   The Z command however displays a blank black raster.

    E> CENTERING CROSSHAIRS  For checking picture centering.

    F> PHOSPHOR IONIZATION   Two white horizontal bars. Leave pattern
                             on screen for 3 minutes. Then check for
                             discoloration of the white bars. This
                             test must be done at full contrast and
                             brightness. If bars are discolored,
                             decrease SUBCONTRAST or evenly decrease
                             R,G,B VIDEO GAIN adjustments using
                             the 16 STEP GRAY SCALE pattern.
                             Phosphor discoloration seems to be
                             caused by either a thermal effect
                             on the phosphor, or more likely,
                             ionization effects which cause a
                             cloud of secondary electrons to form
                             between the front of the tube and the
                             shadow mask (degaussing the tube makes
                             the discoloration disappear immediately).
                             The discoloration also goes away if the
                             brightness or contrast are turned down
                             (takes about 3 or 4 minutes) or the monitor
                             is turned off. Only certain cheaper types
                             of tubes seem to be susceptible to this
                             problem. In any case, the primary cause
                             of the problem is excessive beam current
                             especially when a bright white area is
                             displayed on the screen (such as a Windows
                             menu).


  4> VGA UTILITY MENU
    1> VGA TEST LOOP         Displays a sequence of test patterns.
                             Best used for burn-in testing of monitors
                             that have been repaired. Press any key and
                             wait for the loop to finish to exit. One
                             minor bug: Sometimes when left running
                             overnight, the timing loop that operates
                             the pattern switching sees a high timer
                             count just before midnight, stores that
                             count, and begins looking for it to
                             increment higher. But, because it is
                             midnight, the timer resets, so the loop
                             counter never sees a higher count. This
                             causes the loop to hang while it waits
                             for a higher count (which may not happen
                             at all, or until the next midnight).
                             If this happens, you may need to reboot.

     2> MODE SWITCHING       Checks mode switching of vga monitors
                             between 350-400-480 line modes.

4>MONO MENU      NOTE: MAKE SURE YOU HAVE LOADED MSHERC.COM OR
                       QBHERC.COM BEFORE USING THESE TEST PATTERNS.

    1> FOCUS       Hercules compatible focus pattern.

    2> CROSSHATCH  Hercules compatible crosshatch pattern.
                   Most mono monitors have alignment magnets
                   around the rim of the yoke. If any corners
                   look crooked, adjusting the magnet closest
                   to the problem should correct it.

    3> TEXT        Fills screen with text, with border.

    4> SPLIT SCREEN  Checks intensity switching and contrast control
                   2 large rectangles, one dim, one bright.

    5> WHITE SCREEN  White screen. Used to check brightness and
                   also for phosphor burn. If you can read what
                   the customer does on his monitor, the tube should
                   be replaced.

    6> MULTIBURST  720x348 version of the multiburst test pattern.

    7> MONO TEST LOOP  Provides a test loop for burn-in testing
                   of MONO monitors.

5> HELP    Gives some of the commands for the crosshatch and vga color
           balance patterns.


ADDENDUM:
    VTB 4.5 Freeware has done away with the TIMER loops that caused a midnight
    rollover lockup in the test loops. The TIMER loops have been replaced with
    SLEEP statements that function much better and reduced the size of the EXE by about 12k.
